otype uh  these libraries with this particular  strategy on mind  and in total the seven libraries were  screened and 74 unique hits were  identified  for improvement of cbh1 expression  i also want to  bring your attention to the resources  that were involved um in order to do  this project  so designing a library can take anywhere  from a half an hour to a few hours per  library  the build uh the amount of time you have  to actually interact with the instrument  putting the reagents in your cells on  it's really only a few hours so maybe  two to three hours  per week is spent on that  and then as far as phenotyping and  genotyping  i i estimate it's about a quarter  of an employee  to do this particular workflow so not a  huge amount it's actually a fairly small  amount of resources in order to identify  all of these hits um in this small  period of time  okay so as far as conclusions um kind of  take home messages  are  the onyx platform is really a game  changer for dgtl cycles so it allows you  to rapidly design and build genome  edited libraries using significantly  less resources and it really  democratizes genome editing again i use  myself as an example for the cbh1  project i was able to come up with a  concept design that library build that  library and phenotype it all on my own  without anyone else's  help  i also want to make a point that  genome-wide or untargeted libraries and  targeted or rational libraries can both  identify strains with improved protein  production so we found both of those  strategies as being very fruitful  targeting the expression cassette or  doing rational knockouts as well as  untargeted and unbiased genome-wide  libraries  and also that shallow screening of large  libraries can yield beneficial diversity  in that phenotyping a wide variety of  library concepts is likely better than  deeply screening  a few libraries particularly when you in  the next step when you want to start  combining those since they're um  targeting you know potentially different  bottlenecks within  the protein production pathway  once you start combining those you might  be alleviating multiple bottlenecks and  be able to  obtain strains with significantly  improved  expression  and with that i'd like to thank you for  your time and i'd be happy to take any  questions that you might have  thank you eric for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  okay let's get started  our first question asks  can you upload an in  excuse me can you upload an inscript  designer your genome of interest would  the edit protocol work with organisms or  cells other than  escara asharakiya and saccharomyces  excuse me for the pronunciation  yeah good question so indeed um you can  upload uh whatever genome that you're  interested in or your genome  into the  designer software  the the key there though so you can  upload it and then you can design edits  against that the key is that uh  currently we're only supporting e coli  and um srbca  for that process so you can't just use  whatever cells you want to use um it has  to be a strain that's similar to one of  in scripture's standard strain and there  are kits that we provide that allow you  to actually test the editing process in  your particular string but currently um  e coli and cerebesia are are the two  hosts that we're currently supporting  great thank you our next question asks  genotyping is by sequencing amplicons  yes so there's a couple different  strategies you can you can use you can  either sequence the barcode to identify  um the edits that are contained within  that hit  um or you could do uh whole genome  sequencing so we've used both strategies  um in identifying hits  so both of those are are possible  great thank you  all right next question here your normal  application here is cbp which looks  nothing like mtp or shake flask growth  which library designs are likely to  yield hits that are productive in  application more broadly how important  is it to screen in application relevant  conditions  yeah for sure that i mean that's a very  important point  and you know this is kind of a  scaled-down version of a cbp you know  type process  in that we're really trying to improve  the expression of one enzyme that would  be involved in cbp this is something  that you you if you're actually looking  for um you know  a real uh cbp type hit you would want to  doing this in a in a more complex system  where you're expressing many um other  enzymes this is kind of a simple  simplified case that allows us to you  know use it as a test case um certainly  you would want to  screen as close to application  conditions as you possibly can get i  mean this is something that you know is  very common um that microtiter plate or  small scale  experiments you need to make sure that  those hits go to more application  relevant type processes so that's  something that you have to do um and and  in this case you know we're not actually  trying to build a cbp um strain um we're  just trying to to to show this is an  example of a way to improve um the  expression of an enzyme but that's  that's definitely an important point is  that you want to screen in conditions  that are are most relevant um for your  application or for scale up  wonderful thank you we've got lots of  great questions coming in here the next  one asks is deep screening ever  warranted does the hit analysis tell  when it's worth sampling deeper  yeah so that's that's kind of an  interesting question um  and you know the shallow screening  strategy i can't i think kind of really  depends on what you're trying to get out  of your library right so there might be  an example where you have say a really  targeted library so we have this  targeted knockout library that we wanted  to study and maybe in that case you're  really trying to answer the question of  do these hits have any impact and in  that case you might actually want to  screen quite deeply to make sure that  you've you know assessed all of those  hits and whether or not they they impact  the phenotype that you're looking for  um and then it also depends probably on  um the  the amount of hits that you get out so  if you're able to shallow screen say a  large library and you get a large number  of hits then maybe you're happy with  that number of hits um because kind of  one of the keys that you have to to  think about is you want to identify hits  as fast as you can  which usually yields some level of  improvement but the large level  improvement really comes when you start  combining those  hits into one strain  and this has been seen in you know  protein engineering type of experiments  as well as in strain engineering we have  a nice example of this with lysine  production  where we saw the really large  improvements when we started to combine  those hits  so i think it comes down to something  where what is the phenotyping capacity  you have that's available  how many hits you're identifying  and how fast you want to move because  maybe you just want to identify some  number of hits and then just really  start combining those so it's a good  question and it really depends on what  strategy you're  you're trying to address or your  strategy your phenotyping capacity your  your the speed that you want to work  there's lots of factors that kind of go  into that  decision  good question  great all right next question here how  does the pipeline change for an  adaptively evolved e coli or s  cerevisiae strain  yes so i'm not exactly sure what  they're trying to ask i assume they mean  if you have a strain that you've done  ale  type of work on how does the process  change  and if that's the question that's being  asked so usually you have to do some  sort of genome sequencing of that strain  um so that you can upload that into the  encrypted designer um platform or the i  or encrypted engineering platform so  that you can more accurately design the  edits  against that that strain so ideally you  want to have good sequencing information  i think that that's that's really  critical  all right our next question here says  you haven't had or you haven't yet had a  chance to build the combinatorial  libraries do you have an example of how  high an expression target could be  improvised based on the addition of  positives from several areas of  um  physiology  what would be a reasonable guess as to  how high the activity could go as a  result of combinatorial effort  yeah so that's a difficult um  thing to assess um right now in in this  uh situation i mean we do have examples  in this previous um  application i mentioned we were trying  to overproduce lysine in e coli and we  got a lot of different hits throughout  the genome whether it's targeting the  lysine biosynthetic pathway  or um just genome-wide things that are  targeting basically the genome  everywhere  and what we found in that case was that  we didn't start seeing really large  amounts of improvement until we started  combining them and the greatest  improvement we saw was about a 14 000  fold improvement when we had three  different edits that were combined  within that that strain now that's for a  small molecule so i think that that's  likely not what you're going to see with  the protein production you're not going  to see a 14 000 fold improvement it's  unlikely  um  but i think it's difficult at this point  to really say because um you know we're  starting with probably a very  unoptimized system we're using a wild  type you know  yeast strain that we've inserted a gene  um so not heavily optimized so my  prediction is that when you start  combining those that you're going to get  a much larger level of improvement but  it's hard to say at this point  great thank you looks like we have time  for a few more questions here this next  one asks what is the advantage of using  the inscriptive platform for genome-wide  knockouts over  for example transposon mutagenesis  yeah so um  for transposon mutagenesis uh i believe  those can be targeted i'm not sure that  they can actually be no specifically  targeted they might be able to but i  think um one of the advantages of i  think the inscriptor platform  is that you can specifically target it  um to a location and it doesn't even  have to be to a specific gene you can  target it anywhere um within that gene  um because you have the you know the  opportunity to target so many places  with the gene and you using crispr you  can really engineer the location of  where  that cut's going to be introduced so i  think one of the advantages of this  platform of it is it's really targeted  and then it's also a trackable system so  you can have a barcode that allows you  to very rapidly genotype and track those  edits that are being introduced  great thank you and we have time for one  more question here  can onyx generate random multiple genome  edits in one cell simultaneously  yeah so random um genome edits uh can't  really be introduced i mean it's all  targeted right so  i i guess you would say that's not  necessarily random  um and currently the platform um allows  for the introduction of a single edit at  a time but i will say that we're working  on expanding the platform to be able to  introduce multiple edits at the same  time and in that case you can do maybe  combinatorial type of libraries where  you can have different registers that  allow you to introduce a combination of  different edits simultaneously which  allows you to build you know larger  combinatorial type space or allows you  to sample larger combinatorial space  much greater than a single edit  would be able to introduce  all right well thank you again eric for  your time today and your important  research we would also like to thank lab  roots and our sponsor in scripta for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions questions we  did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  laborats will alert you via email when  it is available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time goodbye
 

